# Operational runbooks. Deliberately NOT in the public repository: they name the
# Safe's signers, carry environment variables and describe key hygiene, which
# published is a targeting package rather than documentation.
# A GLOB, not a list of names. The list was the bug: it named nine files, and
# `docs/LAUNCH_2026_09_12_human.md` and `docs/LAUNCH_2026_12_09.md` were written
# afterwards, matched none of them, and were tracked for publication — one of
# them citing `OPSEC.md` §5quater by section. A new runbook is written under a
# new name every time; the rule has to match the class, not the filenames that
# existed the day it was typed.
docs/LAUNCH*
docs/RELAUNCH.md
docs/relaunch-runbook.html
docs/PAYD_RUNBOOK.md
docs/DEPLOY_HOSTS.md
docs/REHEARSAL.md
docs/WEB_READINESS.md

# A runbook does not stop being one because it is executable. `fly-secrets.sh`
# reads `.env` and stages both hosts' configuration: it names the two Fly apps,
# the private address the keeper reaches the co-signer on, and which variable
# holds which key. That is `docs/DEPLOY_HOSTS.md` in shell, and it is withheld
# for the same reason. It is an operator's tool — nothing public depends on it,
# and nothing about what the contracts do is verified by reading it.
script/fly-secrets.sh

# The internal audit and review record. Eight audit documents and three review
# briefs, ~4 465 lines. They are NOT withheld because they are embarrassing —
# they are the best engineering in the repository. They are withheld because
# three of them are written in the present tense about doors that are still
# open: `AUDIT_FIXES_3.md` §4 "What is deliberately still open", `AUDIT_FIXES_2.md`
# §4 and its "Two findings in the same file that are NOT fixed", and
# `AUDIT_EXECUTION_2.md` §1.4, which spells out a single-key publish path with
# the contract line numbers next to it. Published on launch day that is a map,
# not a credential. `REVIEW_3_SEALED.md` is withheld for a different reason that
# is written at the top of the file itself: it is handed to a reviewer only
# after their own list exists, and a public copy can never be sealed again.
#
# Nothing public depends on them. SECURITY.md states that no audit has been
# done, which stays true, and `docs/README.md` already tells a reader that the
# planning documents are deliberately absent. Re-open the question after the
# residuals close, and if they are published then, publish them as a snapshot
# with the open sections resolved rather than as these files.
docs/AUDIT_PLAN.md
docs/AUDIT_PLAN_2.md
docs/AUDIT_EXECUTION.md
docs/AUDIT_EXECUTION_2.md
docs/AUDIT_FIXES.md
docs/AUDIT_FIXES_2.md
docs/AUDIT_FIXES_3.md
docs/AUDIT_PAYD.md
docs/REVIEW_3.md
docs/REVIEW_3_AI.md
docs/REVIEW_3_SEALED.md

# Internal planning and strategy. Not secret, but not product documentation
# either: PLAN.md carries deliberation and options that were never shipped, and a
# published ROADMAP reads as a set of commitments made by an identifiable team —
# which is the one thing a protocol's documentation should never do. BRAND.md is
# the naming ledger. All three stay on the maintainers' disks.
PLAN.md
ROADMAP.md
docs/ROADMAP.md
BRAND.md
ESTIMATIONS.md
LAUNCH_2026-09-12.md

# The assistant's working file. Its ENGINEERING rules live in the repository as
# docs/CONVENTIONS.md; what stays here is the part addressed to a collaborator
# rather than to the code.
CLAUDE.md


# v1 production artifacts. They carry real holder addresses and belong on a
# disk, not in a public repository.
offchain/final-artifact-*.json

# The personal denylist the pre-commit hook reads. Never committed: a tracked
# file listing the names it blocks would publish them.
.githooks/identity.local

# The three files that carry the wallets, the accepted third-party links, the
# dated reasoning behind each structuring choice, and the list to run before
# anything becomes public. None of them is a secret; all of them are a targeting
# package if published together.
OPSEC.md
DECISIONS.md
PREFLIGHT.md

# Local tooling and scratch work. Nothing here is part of the product, and
# nothing here needs to be read to verify what the contracts do.
.private/

# Editor and agent configuration. `squash-publish.sh` publishes `SRC^{tree}`
# VERBATIM, so anything committed here would ship in the public repository —
# tooling choices included. Nothing in it is part of the product.
.claude/

# Coverage output. The CI job publishes it as an artifact; it does not belong
# in the tree.
lcov.info
